SUMMARY:TEC Seminar: Kim Sarnoff\, Caltech - “ The structure of sequent
 ial updating”
LOCATION:North Hall 2113
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Kim Sarnoff\, Caltech\nTitle: “ The structure of s
 equential updating”\n\nMany real-world inference problems unfold over 
 time: employers learn about workers’ ability across tasks\, consumers 
 evaluate products through repeated use\, and policymakers revise beliefs
  about the economy as they observe new data. For a Bayesian\, beliefs ev
 olve according to the principle of posterior sufficiency: the posterior 
 summarizes past information\, so all one needs for the next update is th
 e current belief. Despite its theoretical and practical import\, experim
 ental work on dynamic updating has largely neglected this property. This
  paper experimentally tests posterior sufficiency and studies mechanisms
  for its violation. Three main results emerge. First\, sufficiency fails
  almost universally. Second\, these violations can be explained with non
 -Bayesian responses to two features of sequences: the order in which inf
 ormation arrives and the raw count of signals. Third\, violations of suf
 ficiency are larger in magnitude than belief distortions in static updat
 ing problems.\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/tec-seminar-kim-s
 arnoff-caltech
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/tec-seminar-kim-sarnoff-caltech
CATEGORIES:TEC
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
